al author and source are credited.</license-p></license></permissions><related-article related-article-type="corrected-article" ext-link-type="doi" xlink:href="10.7554/elife.100002" id="ra1"/><kwd-group kwd-group-type="research-organism"><title>Research organism</title><kwd>Human</kwd><kwd>Mouse</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front><body><boxed-text><p>Fei H, Lu X, Shi Z, Liu X, Yang C, Zhu X, Lin Y, Jiang Z, Wang J, Huang D, Liu L, Zhang S, Jiang L. 2025. Deciphering the preeclampsia-specific immune microenvironment and the role of pro-inflammatory macrophages at the maternal–fetal interface. <italic>eLife</italic> <bold>13</bold>:RP100002. doi: <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://doi.org/10.7554/elife.100002">10.7554/elife.100002</ext-link>.</p><p>Published 28 March 2025</p></boxed-text><p>In the published article, an error was identified in the TIM-3 expression data shown in the lower panel of Figure 2E and the associated Figure 2—source data 1. During compilation of the source data file, values from the gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) group were inadvertently copied into columns assigned to the normal pregnancy (NP) group, replacing the original NP values. The lower panel of Figure 2E was generated from this incorrectly compiled dataset.</p><p>The error was identified during a subsequent verification of the original experimental records. The independent original measurements for the NP and GDM groups were retrieved from the original data files provided by PLTTECH Service Co, Ltd (Hangzhou, China). The TIM-3 data were reanalysed using the corrected NP measurements, and the lower panel of Figure 2E and the corresponding Figure 2—source data 1 have been corrected.</p><p>The corrected analysis confirms that TIM-3 expression is significantly lower in the preeclampsia (PE) group than in the NP group. It also shows significantly lower TIM-3 expression in the GDM&amp;PE group than in the NP group, whereas this comparison was previously reported as not significant. The PD-1 expression data and statistical results in the upper panel of Figure 2E are unchanged.</p><p>The relevant text in the Results section has therefore been corrected as follows:</p><p><bold>Corrected text in Results:</bold></p><p>Moreover, lower expression of immune checkpoint molecules including T-cell immunoglobulin mucin-3 (Tim-3) and programmed cell death 1 (PD-1) was found in CD45RA<sup>−</sup>CCR7<sup>+</sup>CD4<sup>+</sup> memory T cells in the PE group, suggesting that these cells have a lower immunosuppressive capacity (Chen et al., 2022b; Fanelli et al., 2021; Rasmussen et al., 2022; Wang et al., 2016). TIM-3 expression was also significantly lower in the GDM&amp;PE group than in the NP group, whereas no significant difference was observed in TIM-3 expression in the GDM group or in PD-1 expression in either the GDM or GDM&amp;PE group compared with the NP group (Figure 2E).</p><p><bold>Originally published text in Results:</bold></p><p>Moreover, lower expression of immune checkpoint molecules including T-cell immunoglobulin mucin-3 (Tim-3) and programmed cell death 1 (PD-1) was found in CD45RA<sup>−</sup>CCR7<sup>+</sup>CD4<sup>+</sup> memory T cells in the PE group, suggesting that these cells have a lower immunosuppressive capacity (Chen et al., 2022b; Fanelli et al., 2021; Rasmussen et al., 2022; Wang et al., 2016), there was no significant difference in the GDM or GDM&amp;PE groups comparing with the NP group (Figure 2E).</p><p><bold>Corrected Figure 2E:</bold></p><fig id="fig1" position="float"><graphic mimetype="image/tiff" xlink:href="elife-113687-fig1-v1.tif"/></fig><p><bold>Originally published Figure 2E:</bold></p><fig id="fig2" position="float"><graphic mimetype="image/tiff" xlink:href="elife-113687-fig2-v1.tif"/></fig><p><bold>Corrected Figure 2E source data:</bold></p><table-wrap id="inlinetable1" position="anchor"><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" valign="top">TIM3</th><th align="left" valig